\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 Hi, Miguel\
\'a0\
Attached is the updated quotaiton of H type chicken cages, the light system price has included.\
\'a0\
Regarding your question about why there are light bulbs and only in the hallway, the above is the response I received after consulting our engineers.\
\'a0\
1.Installing lights inside the cages is more specific to furnished/ welfare cages, and they are usually LED light strips rather than bulbs. The light is used to simulate sunlight, promoting the chickens' feeding, drinking, and egg-laying.\
\'a0\
2.H-type layer cages are originally designed for high-density, centralized farming within the cages, pursuing economic benefits rather than animal welfare. They have a compact structure, low overall height, and limited space. If lights were to be installed inside them, firstly there would be no space, and secondly there would be hidden risks: the chickens might peck at the lights out of boredom, and water line leaks inside the cage could cause electrical conduction. Installing them in the aisle also makes maintenance and inspection easier.\
\'a0\
3.Regarding his concern that "there will not be significant attenuation of light intensity between the top and bottom tiers," as a supplier of integrated farming equipment with decades of experience, we can absolutely arrange the bulbs in a staggered high-low pattern according to the layout, with a spacing of about 2.5 meters. There is no need to worry about light attenuation. In response to your question about why only the aisle has bulbs, the above is the conclusion I reached after consulting our engineer.\
\'a0\
The Specification of bulb is DC48V-7W-3000K.\
\'a0\
Please share if you have further questions.\
\'a0\
